-- allium: 3
-- For epoch-scoped keys, the confirmed leader supplies the retained catalog, replay, pin and admitted compaction roots under its term.
-- The EpochScopedStorage contract in ../../allium/rtdb-multi.allium governs these keys in default and Multi modes.
-- A default applied-branch attachment with object custody allocates each checkpoint image in the producing replica term.
-- On coordinated local storage the confirmed leader holds exclusive recovery custody while it collects older-epoch images outside the retained block roots.
-- A recovered branch view keeps its decoded image in memory for the lifetime of the view.
-- gc.allium
--
-- Garbage collection of superseded tries (data + meta files) for one database.
--
-- Per-database, leader-only, signal-driven.
-- The node that leads this database's replica log owns its trie GC.
-- The leader signals at block adoption and transaction processing continues without waiting for the cycle.
-- In native Rust, the term supervisor also signals on term entry and periodically while automatic trie GC is enabled.
-- The period is the configured reader lease clamped to 100 milliseconds through 60 seconds.
-- A retry gives an eligible trie or pending receipt a fresh collection opportunity even if no new block arrives.
-- The timer only schedules cycles. The collector still checks eligibility, elapsed reader leases and the catalog admission floor.
-- In installed-journal mode, a storage delete request queues the owned serving object for reclamation under journal custody.
--
-- Tries transition to `garbage` during addTries (see trie-cat.allium); this
-- spec covers physical cleanup: deleting object-store files and publishing
-- those deletions to the replica log so followers drop them from their
-- in-memory catalogs at the same logical position.
--
-- Native collection within one partition authority captures eligibility and reader references together.
-- A trie that is live in any captured reader catalog stays out of the collection batch.
-- At the same cut, the native collector excludes tries recorded as live or nascent in the latest installed checkpoint.
-- Block adoption installs the replacement protection from the persisted table blocks together with the new block index.
-- Pending tries remain required because a retained replica notice can complete their compaction cohort after recovery.
-- Query and transaction snapshots register at the partition owner's state boundary.
-- An admitted provider operation retains that registration through completion, including after caller cancellation.
-- Query execution carries the selected storage registration through provider admission and checks its lease before returning a memory or provider answer.
-- Compactor reset starts a new naming epoch, so a deletion authorized before it can never reach an output planned after it.
--
-- Query and fragment snapshots on coordinated storage register with the catalog floor before storage access.
-- An admitted capture retains that registration through its provider requests.
-- The captures of one catalog version through one buffer pool share one registration, which ends when the last of them ends.
-- Each capture that joins a held registration is admitted against the floor exactly as a new registration would be.
-- A database holds its own share of the registration for the cut its partition publishes, from that cut's first capture until it publishes another.
-- Compaction and stored artefact builds register their input catalogs on coordinated storage.
-- A fallback build registers the catalog it recaptures for its own reads.
-- Replicated snapshots on uncoordinated storage hold a bounded read lease.
-- A node anchors its lease at a monotonic clock reading taken before it reads the replica log's tip, once its catalog has applied that tip.
-- A node confirms any pending observation its catalog has already applied before deciding whether to sample the tip again.
-- A snapshot captured after that confirmation may begin provider requests strictly before the anchor plus `reader_lease` minus `reader_lease_margin`.
-- Each provider request checks the lease when it is admitted; a request admitted in time runs to completion.
-- Objects are immutable, so a request that overruns the deadline can only fail; a missing object it then reports is classified as the expired lease.
-- A node reading a scan fragment for another node's query captures its own snapshot and holds its own lease.
-- A later request fails with `rtdb.storage/read-lease-expired` rather than reading a file that may be gone.
-- A node whose catalog cannot be confirmed current waits up to the margin for its log processor.
-- A replicated query or delegated fragment without a current lease is refused at snapshot admission.
-- An admitted snapshot still checks the lease before beginning each provider request.
-- The native collector deletes an eligible trie only once `reader_lease` has passed on its own monotonic clock since it first observed that trie as eligible.
-- In Multi, an installed trie release can settle as soon as its floor, recovery coverage and provider publication authorize retirement.
-- Path releases and checkpoint pack collection still use a separate grace.
-- Local Multi can also erase an original L0 pack under independent replay archive custody.
-- That erasure retains logical L0 descriptors so ordered-index replay can resolve the same inputs.
-- Native nodes with a local journal and local storage share a catalog floor at the storage root.
-- Catalog admission and floor advancement use the same filesystem lock.
-- A reader's shared ticket lock retains its catalog until its last provider operation completes or its process exits.
-- The collector submits an installed receipt to the storage owner.
-- The classic grant binds that receipt's immutable file identities to the storage owner.
-- The current conservative decision persists the admission floor before issuing the grant.
-- A native local trie deletion grant holds the recovery gate exclusively through the physical deletion.
-- A startup hold shares that gate through checkpoint discovery and cursor admission.
-- A capture in the floor's journal must supply a cut at or above the durable floor.
-- A successor journal can open from the latest durable checkpoint after offline replacement.
-- Held catalogs from the predecessor and opaque captures delay the successor floor.
-- Installing the successor floor fences earlier journals.
-- Classic remote storage orders holds and floor advancement on one conditional-write object in the storage root.
-- The object sequence advances even when rescinding an absent hold after an uncertain registration.
-- A remote hold remains through its last admitted provider request.
-- Loss of the holder without a drain retains its ticket.
-- An exclusive compactor reset resumes cut publication after it adopts its new naming epoch.
-- The installed prefix covers the new catalog before the reset completes.
-- A coordinated reset stages the compacted file identities with the successor block and naming epoch before adoption.
-- Reset intents can publish retirement only after the adopted checkpoint covers those coordinates and the selected trie names have been withdrawn.
-- Collection resumes pending reset and ordinary retirements before selecting new age-eligible garbage.
-- The native leader acknowledges a trie removal after its known installed replica prefix covers the removal record.
-- The immutable partition value at acknowledgement contains neither a requested key nor a checkpoint dependency on that key.
-- A skipped replay record or an unrecognized prefix cannot supply this acknowledgement.
-- When its partition has an installed catalog cut, the native collector persists an intent for each batch before releasing files or submitting the removal.
-- It records a later receipt from that journal before releasing the intent marker.
-- In Multi the serving writer proposes that installed cut as the admission floor for its storage root.
-- If an older or opaque catalog is still held, the marker stays pending until a successor can install or observe the floor.
-- A hosted trie batch queues its files only after the durable receipt and floor cover the same installed cut.
-- The release operation loads the installed receipt by its exact plan digest.
-- Its release records carry the residence, object namespace and installed cut.
-- The cell reaper admits the release when its directory observation carries that residence and namespace and covers the installed cut in the same storage root and journal.
-- The published recovery inventory must cover the installed removal.
-- Older recovery and metadata admissions retain the released files until their provider work drains.
-- A coordinated receipt L0 release requires a successor omitting the compacted input.
-- Directory custody authenticates the exact publication and uses the certified replay anchor to exclude the removal position.
-- On Classic local storage, the same grant owner requires the retained repair anchor and the exclusive recovery gate before deleting a receipt L0.
-- Offline classic log-prefix settlement compares the retained root and locks both journal files.
-- It overwrites covered payloads in place and disposes of skipped diagnostics through the selected source position.
-- Files in that plan release in artefact, data and metadata order.
-- An interrupted release retains the plan so a successor can retry the files before releasing the intent marker.
-- Hosted collection waits for an installed catalog cut before selecting a batch.
-- The floor is a bound on future reader admission; physical erasure continues through the timed release path.
-- An observed version of a loose data or metadata file matches the publication only when it equals the version the selected catalog recorded.
-- A file without a matching publication remains eligible for collection through the existing timed path.
-- A new collector checks pending intents in the same storage root and journal.
-- Where the installed catalog and checkpoint already exclude the requested keys, it can re-confirm their removal through replication.
-- Source-residence retirement metadata stays outside fork publication and cell materialization.
-- A new collector, after restart or failover, observes afresh.
-- Both sides measure elapsed time, so clocks need no synchronization; the margin covers twice the rate drift over one lease plus the longest provider request.
--
-- Each cycle first retires the tables of owned relations whose retention has passed (rule RetireDueTables).
-- Their tries then become garbage like any superseded trie, and the deletion rules below collect them unchanged.
-- Durable logical search staging requires a recoverable writer registration before uploading.
-- A directory-managed holder records its writer epoch and provider region before uploading.
-- A replacement holder can order abandonment once the writer epoch has advanced and the originating region has drained and left the directory record.
-- A coordinated Classic local writer records a durable filesystem witness before uploading.
-- The collector requires the witness to exist and its exclusive lock to be available before taking over an abandoned upload.
-- Classic remote storage declines logical search staging until it can supply recoverable writer drain evidence.
-- Hosted retirement of an installed search run requires its immutable provider publication and catalog grant.
--
-- Block-file GC lives in block-gc.allium — block files are consumed only at
-- startup/catch-up, so they don't need replica-log coordination.
--
-- See:
--   garbage_collector/TrieGarbageCollector.kt
--   indexer/GarbageCollector.kt (commitTriesDeleted, triesDeleted)
--   indexer/LeaderLogProcessor.kt (TriesDeleted apply, block-adopt signal)
--   indexer/FollowerLogProcessor.kt (TriesDeleted replay)
--   api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (TriesDeleted)
--   api/GarbageCollectorConfig.kt

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat
use "./db.allium" as db

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    database: db/Database
    log_processor: db/LogProcessor
    table_catalog: db/TableCatalog
    trie_gc: TrieGarbageCollector
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ObjectStore (durable key-value storage owning trie data + meta files keyed
-- by (table, trie_key); deletes are idempotent — 404 on a re-DELETE is fine)
-- is reached via the ObjectStoreDelete deferred specification.


entity TrieGarbageCollector {
    -- One per (database, leader-system) pair. Constructed when the
    -- LogProcessor enters a leader mode (db.allium: TransitionToLeader) and
    -- closed when it leaves (TransitionToFollower or node shutdown). Followers
    -- never construct one. See TrieGarbageCollector.kt.
    --
    -- The collector owns a single coroutine that loops: wait → run a cycle →
    -- (drain new triggers) → run another cycle → ... . Two channels feed the
    -- loop:
    --   signal_channel:  fire-and-forget pokes (CONFLATED — bursts coalesce
    --                    into one upcoming cycle).
    --   await_channel:   one CompletableDeferred per awaiter (UNLIMITED —
    --                    every waiter must be preserved so each gets a cycle
    --                    that *started* after their suspension).
    --
    -- See `TrieGarbageCollector.kt` for the loop body.
    enabled: Boolean              -- gates automatic signals; awaitNoGarbage is unaffected
    blocks_to_keep: Integer
    garbage_lifetime: Duration

    has_pending_signal: Boolean
    pending_awaiters: List<Awaiter>

    -- The coroutine context that runs garbage_collect_tries. Owned by the
    -- leader system; cancelled and joined on close().
    is_running: Boolean

    -- Derived: the loop body fires whenever either channel has work.
    has_work: has_pending_signal or pending_awaiters.count > 0
}

value Awaiter {
    -- A single suspended caller of awaitNoGarbage. Resolved (or cancelled) by
    -- the GC loop after a cycle that started at or after the call.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Replica-log message
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Published by the leader's trie GC after deleting (data, meta) files in
-- object store; consumed by followers (and by the transition processor) to
-- mirror the deletion in their in-memory trie catalogs. In the implementation
-- this is a variant of db/ReplicaMessage; we model it as a local entity to
-- keep ownership scoped to this spec.
-- See: api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (TriesDeleted)
entity TriesDeletedMessage {
    table_name: String
    trie_keys: List<String>
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- See: api/GarbageCollectorConfig.kt
    -- Defaults match the implementation.
    enabled: Boolean = true
    blocks_to_keep: Integer = 10
    garbage_lifetime: Duration = 24.hours

    -- Caps the number of trie keys per `TriesDeleted` message so a single
    -- replica-log append stays under Kafka's `max.message.bytes` (1 MB by
    -- default). Months of accumulated backlog on a hot table can produce tens
    -- of thousands of garbage trie keys; one message per chunk keeps each
    -- append small.
    -- See: TrieGarbageCollector.kt (TRIES_DELETED_CHUNK_SIZE)
    tries_deleted_chunk_size: Integer = 1024
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The trie GC's lifetime is bound to the leader term: constructed when the log
-- processor enters Leader mode (see db.allium TransitionToLeader) and closed when
-- it leaves. One rule, because there is one leader mode — whether the database is
-- CDC-sourced makes no difference to the GC.

rule LeaderOpensTrieGc {
    -- See: indexer/LeaderLogProcessor.kt (init)
    when: lp: db/LogProcessor.mode becomes Leader

    ensures:
        TrieGarbageCollector.created(
            enabled: config.enabled,
            blocks_to_keep: config.blocks_to_keep,
            garbage_lifetime: config.garbage_lifetime,
            has_pending_signal: false,
            is_running: true
        )
}

rule LeaderClosesTrieGc {
    -- Cancels the GC loop. Any in-flight cycle is cancelled; pending awaiters
    -- have their deferreds cancelled (their awaitNoGarbage call throws
    -- CancellationException).
    --
    -- A chunk whose `TriesDeleted` this term appended but has not yet read back fails with the term, as does one still queued behind it: nothing may be left awaiting a term that has gone, and the symptom of missing one is a hang rather than an error (db.allium: Transition Safety).
    --
    -- Fires when the log processor leaves a leader mode for Follower (revoke)
    -- or Transition (intermediate state during handoff).
    -- See: indexer/LeaderLogProcessor.kt (close), TrieGarbageCollector.close
    when: lp: db/LogProcessor.mode transitions_to Follower
    requires: trie_gc.is_running

    ensures:
        trie_gc.is_running = false
        for awaiter in trie_gc.pending_awaiters:
            CancelAwaiter(awaiter)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Triggering
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Two channels feed the loop, each playing a different role:
--
--   `signal()`:           fire-and-forget. Used by the leader once it has adopted
--                         a block (db.allium: LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock). The channel is
--                         conflated, so a burst of block boundaries coalesces
--                         into one upcoming cycle. Tx processing never blocks on
--                         GC progress — the first cycle after a long disabled
--                         period could take minutes.
--
--   `awaitNoGarbage()`:   suspending. Used by tests and admin pokes
--                         (Database.gcAll). Every caller is enqueued into an
--                         UNLIMITED channel and resumed only after a cycle
--                         that started at or after their call has completed.
--                         If two awaiters arrive while a cycle is running,
--                         BOTH are guaranteed to see a fresh cycle — the loop
--                         drains its triggers after each cycle and runs again
--                         if any are pending.
--
-- The enabled flag gates signals from block adoption and the native term timer.
-- Direct `awaitNoGarbage()` always runs a cycle, regardless of `enabled`.

rule LeaderFinishBlockSignalsTrieGc {
    -- Block boundaries are the only moments where new tries become garbage
    -- (compaction supersedes inputs as part of an addTries triggered by a
    -- block flush — see trie-cat.allium). After every block upload, the
    -- leader pokes the trie GC so any backlog gets cleaned up promptly.
    --
    -- Signalled ON the leader's persister coroutine, which is also what will
    -- later run `commit_tries_deleted`. Safe because `signal` is non-suspending
    -- and only enqueues: it cannot re-enter the persister, so it cannot deadlock
    -- against the cycle it is scheduling.
    --
    -- Chains from db.allium's LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock, i.e. once the leader has read its own upload back and moved onto the next block — not when the boundary was cut.
    when: TrieGcSignalled()
    requires: trie_gc.enabled
    requires: trie_gc.is_running

    ensures:
        trie_gc.has_pending_signal = true
}

rule AdminTriggersTrieGc {
    -- Tests and admin code call Database.gcAll(), which awaits one cycle of
    -- both block GC and trie GC. Bypasses the `enabled` gate.
    -- See: database/Database.kt (gcAll), indexer/LogProcessor.kt (gcAll)
    when: AdminAwaitsNoTrieGarbage(caller, database)
    requires: trie_gc.is_running

    ensures:
        let awaiter = Awaiter.created()
        trie_gc.pending_awaiters.add(awaiter)
        AwaiterSuspends(awaiter)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Cycle execution
------------------------------------------------------------

rule TrieGcLoopRunsCycle {
    -- The loop body. Driven by either a signal or an awaiter arriving on the
    -- corresponding channel.
    --
    -- Two structural guarantees the implementation upholds (see the loop in
    -- TrieGarbageCollector.kt for the coroutine structure):
    --
    --   1. Every awaiter sees a cycle that *started* after their suspension.
    --      The loop drains both channels into the current cycle's pending
    --      list, runs the cycle, then re-checks both channels in
    --      `drainTriggers`. If anything has arrived while we ran, we loop and
    --      run again — that next cycle started post-arrival, so its waiters
    --      are satisfied.
    --
    --   2. Awaiters never get stranded by a crashing cycle. On cycle failure,
    --      every pending awaiter has `completeExceptionally(e)` invoked.
    --      Cancellation propagates to all pending awaiters too.
    when: gc: TrieGarbageCollector.has_work
    requires: trie_gc.is_running

    ensures:
        GarbageCollectTries(garbage_as_of: null)
        for awaiter in trie_gc.pending_awaiters:
            CompleteAwaiter(awaiter)
        trie_gc.has_pending_signal = false
        for awaiter in trie_gc.pending_awaiters:
            trie_gc.pending_awaiters.remove(awaiter)
}

rule GarbageCollectTries {
    -- Run one cycle: collect garbage from every table in the table catalog.
    -- The same cycle releases superseded logical search runs once the current checkpoint and all admitted readers have moved past their receipts.
    -- Stores without exact search-run retirement wait for the collection lease to elapse.
    -- Hosted release binds the provider publication to a catalog floor before acknowledgement.
    -- Entity-directory collection marks all pages reachable from the current root, retained checkpoints, captured readers, and retired roots still within their reader lease.
    -- After its reader lease, a retired root can release its unmarked pages.
    -- Hosted fork and archive holds continue to protect the pages in their checkpoint closures.
    -- A replay checkpoint holds every page reached by each active entity directory.
    -- Term-directory pages have no retirement ledger: collection releases a stored page that no current, checkpointed or captured root reaches, once the reader lease has passed since it was first seen unreachable.
    -- Each term-directory build gives newly written pages distinct digests so a rebuild cannot revive a page selected by a concurrent sweep.
    -- Hosted release of a retired search run, an exact sealed artefact, or a directory page waits for an open lineage, drained fork preparations, and the withdrawal of declared fork references to the publication.
    -- A hosted directory page release requires an observed immutable provider publication.
    -- An archive copy or admitted reference read retains a publication while its storage region holds it.
    -- A version-scoped archive hold permits release when the publication belongs to the hold's provider namespace and falls outside the hold.
    -- The hold covers the exact provider key and version for a versioned publication and the provider key for a terminal publication.
    -- The collector leaves a held run or page pending for a later cycle.
    -- The reaper validates the current holds again under its physical retirement reservation.
    -- Tables are processed concurrently up to `tableParallelism`, with DELETE
    -- operations bounded across the cycle by `deleteParallelism`. Both bounds
    -- are out-of-spec implementation parameters (see TrieGarbageCollector.kt).
    --
    -- Failures in one table are logged but don't abort the cycle —
    -- supervisorScope isolates them.
    when: GarbageCollectTries(garbage_as_of)

    let as_of = garbage_as_of ?? default_garbage_as_of()

    -- If fewer than `blocks_to_keep` blocks exist, default_garbage_as_of
    -- returns null and the cycle is a no-op.
    requires: as_of != null

    ensures:
        for table in table_catalog.all_tables:
            GarbageCollectTable(table, as_of)
}

-- Returns the system_time of the block at `blocks_to_keep` positions back from
-- latest, minus `garbage_lifetime`. This window is the grace period during
-- which a superseded trie is still allowed to be referenced by an in-flight
-- query. Returns null if fewer than `blocks_to_keep` blocks exist.
-- See: TrieGarbageCollector.defaultGarbageAsOf

rule GarbageCollectTable {
    -- Per-table: ask the trie catalog which keys are garbage as of `as_of`,
    -- then process them in chunks. Within a chunk, the order is mandatory:
    --
    --   (1) DELETE every (data, meta) file in object store.
    --   (2) AND THEN publish `TriesDeleted` to the replica log, on which every node —
    --       this one included — removes the keys from its trie catalog.
    --
    -- The reverse order would leave followers (and the leader's own catalog)
    -- thinking the deleted files were still live — unsafe if a query tried to
    -- read them.
    --
    -- A crash mid-cycle leaves orphaned catalog entries pointing at deleted
    -- files. Recovery: the next cycle re-asks the catalog (those entries are
    -- still flagged garbage), re-DELETEs (S3 returns 404, idempotent), and
    -- re-publishes. No special crash recovery code; the design is naturally
    -- idempotent.
    when: GarbageCollectTable(table, as_of)

    -- The catalog's garbage-trie calculation is treated as a black box by
    -- this spec — see trie-cat.allium for the rules that mark tries garbage.
    let garbage_keys = trie_cat/trie_catalog.garbageTries(table, as_of)

    requires: garbage_keys.count > 0

    ensures:
        for chunk in chunk_by(garbage_keys, config.tries_deleted_chunk_size):
            -- Order matters within a chunk; chunks are processed sequentially.
            DeleteAndCommitChunk(table, chunk)
}

rule DeleteAndCommitChunkRule {
    -- This order applies to classic stores, where the collector issues the file deletes itself.
    -- In Multi, the recorded file plan instead releases to the reaper after receipt and floor installation as described above.
    -- An installed plan that matches an immutable S3 publication releases its exact version to the hosted reaper.
    -- A terminal plan carries the publication identity recorded by the file writer into its installed release.
    -- The hosted reaper compares that identity inside the enrolled allocation before closing the owner's read admission for the file and release record.
    -- Existing shared manifests and active physical owner reads retain that version until their dependencies are withdrawn or drained.
    -- An older hosted marker can recover its immutable version from the authenticated installed receipt for the same cut and object.
    -- One atomic step in the per-table per-chunk ordering. The DELETEs run
    -- first, in parallel; only after every DELETE in the chunk has succeeded
    -- do we publish + commit.
    --
    -- File order within a (data, meta) pair: data first, meta last. The meta
    -- file is the "completion marker" for a trie — deleting it last keeps the
    -- pair from ever transiently looking complete-but-empty to a concurrent
    -- reader.
    --
    -- Any DELETE failure aborts the chunk before the publish happens.
    -- Followers must never drop catalog entries for files still on disk.
    --
    -- A completed pending search upload may be abandoned after its source cut is installed and the staging owner has drained.
    -- The collector uses a fresh observation to bind the completed body to its provider publication where the store requires one.
    -- Hosted drain requires a successor writer epoch and removal of the staged writer's storage region.
    -- Native local drain requires acquiring the staged attachment's witness lock.
    -- A fresh provider absence observed after drain settles an intent with no uploaded body.
    -- Ordered abandonment succeeds while the allocation is unadopted and fences later adoption at the same identity.
    -- A hosted release of an installed retirement requires the catalog admission floor and proof of the provider version or terminal publication.
    -- The ordered release records the replica position at which the abandoned allocation settled.
    -- A checkpoint preserves the released abandonment until collection orders its removal.
    -- Collection requires the oldest recoverable checkpoint boundary to follow settlement, and a fresh observation that the drained writer's staging intent is absent.
    -- Open catalog captures and local recovery admissions can retain an older view.
    -- Classic local collection holds the recovery gate exclusively through durable publication of the prune to the source log.
    -- The collector releases the gate before waiting for ordered application.
    -- Directory-page collection precedes search tombstone pruning within a table's sweep.
    -- Hosted collection checks the catalog admission floor, authenticated recovery coverage, and fork and archive holds before pruning.
    -- A released abandonment from an older checkpoint format gains a settlement position through a repeated ordered release once its staging intent is absent.
    -- Replay checkpoint copies and archives include every authenticated page reached by the recorded term directory roots.
    -- A serving receipt over a complete semantic entity directory omits the covered obsolete L0 pairs, logical posting runs, and term directory pages.
    -- The lane rebuilds the search files from the entity directory and the surviving primary payloads.
    -- Replay copies retain the obsolete L0 pairs required by reset and relocation.
    -- Term directory pages can be shared across catalog generations, and survive retirement of individual posting runs.
    -- A trie's artefacts, such as its search files (fts.allium), go before its data file: they carry the data file's content, so they must not outlive it.
    -- They are found by listing the trie's artefact prefix rather than by reading its entry's records, which also catches an artefact whose record had not been applied when this cycle read its snapshot.
    -- A partition group's artefacts (trie-cat.allium: ArtefactScope) are derived from every member's data file, so they go once the cycle collects the last member the catalog holds, before any chunk.
    -- A recorded shared file binds to an observed provider version only when at least one retained record names it and every retained record for that file witnesses that same publication.
    -- A member the catalog holds and this cycle does not collect, live or not yet past its garbage lifetime, keeps them.
    -- A search pack follows the same retention rule using the member list in its object name.
    -- finished_pack_files selects packs whose held members are all eligible and whose final held member is in the current deletion chunk.
    -- A sealed search file on a garbage level-0 trie can retire when neither a checkpoint nor a reader needs that trie and the read lease has elapsed.
    -- Its primary data and metadata retain their recovery custody.
    -- See: maintenance/src/gc.rs (collect_table, finished_groups)
    when: DeleteAndCommitChunk(table, chunk)

    ensures:
        for key in finished_pack_files(table, chunk):
            ObjectStoreDelete(key)
        for group in finished_groups(table, chunk):
            for key in ObjectStoreList(trie_cat/group_artefact_prefix(table, group)):
                ObjectStoreDelete(key)
        for trie_key in chunk:
            for key in ObjectStoreList(trie_cat/artefact_prefix(table, trie_key)):
                ObjectStoreDelete(key)
            ObjectStoreDelete(data_file_path(table, trie_key))
            ObjectStoreDelete(meta_file_path(table, trie_key))

        CommitTriesDeleted(table, chunk)
}

rule CommitTriesDeletedRule {
    -- The append is one task on the leader's persister coroutine: the GC hands `commitTriesDeleted` to that coroutine and awaits it, rather than appending itself.
    --
    -- What the await has to rule out is a table-block file disagreeing with the replica log it claims to snapshot:
    --
    --   1. `TriesDeleted(G)` is appended at replica position N.
    --   2. A block boundary at replica position M > N produces a block, whose upload writes table-block files snapshotting the current catalog.
    --   3. If the catalog still contained G at step 2, that file records "catalog includes G" while the log already says G is gone.
    --
    -- A cold start hydrating from M would then see G in its catalog, pointing at object-store files already deleted.
    -- Log order closes it: both the removal and the block that snapshots it are applied on read-back, at N and at M respectively, so a snapshot is downstream of every removal appended ahead of it.
    --
    -- See: indexer/GarbageCollector.kt (commitTriesDeleted, triesDeleted), db.allium LeaderAppliesOwnTriesDeleted.
    when: CommitTriesDeleted(table, trie_keys)

    ensures:
        TriesDeletedMessage.created(
            table_name: table.schema_and_table,
            trie_keys: trie_keys
        )

        -- Applied on the leader's read-back of that record, where this await ends — so the catalog no longer lists the keys by the time the chunk returns.
        trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(table, trie_keys)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Retired relations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Published by the leader's trie GC; every node, the leader included on read-back, applies it to its trie catalog.
-- See: replica-log.proto (TablesRetired)
entity TablesRetiredMessage {
    table_names: List<String>
    as_of: Instant
}

rule RetireDueTables {
    -- A hosted Root commit retires an owned relation at a transaction, with a retention (tx.allium).
    -- An adopted successor retains its original relation identity for ordered erasure until the predecessor's physical history can be reclaimed.
    -- A successor erase advances both release frontiers, including the predecessor's while an admitted hold can still reference retired files.
    -- At the start of each cycle, before selecting garbage, the collector reads every principal's retired catalog at the applied present.
    -- A relation is due once its retirement time plus retention is at or before that present, and no selectable world with an earlier basis in the same principal's catalog names it in its schemas.
    -- A world remains selectable strictly before its expiry and while it has not been dropped. The catalog hold keeps already admitted dependencies through drain.
    -- Its tables are its data, change journal, unique, capacity, fixed-range, purge, purge-floor and lifetime tables that the table catalog holds and that are not retired yet.
    -- World overlays of the relation stay outside: they go with their world.
    -- The collector publishes the due tables in chunks of tries_deleted_chunk_size, each with the applied present as its retirement time, and awaits each record's application.
    -- The cycle that publishes still collects from the catalog it captured before, so the newly garbage tries wait for a later cycle.
    -- From then on they are garbage like any superseded trie: the eligibility, reader and checkpoint protection, reader lease and DeleteAndCommitChunk rules apply unchanged.
    -- L0 recovery files of a retired table stay, as every table's do.
    -- A failure is reported to the observer and leaves the rest of the cycle running.
    -- See: maintenance/src/gc.rs (retire_due_tables), node/src/table_retirement.rs
    when: RetireDueTables(present)

    let due = retired_relation_tables(present)

    requires: due.count > 0

    ensures:
        for chunk in chunk_by(due, config.tries_deleted_chunk_size):
            TablesRetiredMessage.created(table_names: chunk, as_of: present)
            for table in chunk:
                trie_cat/RetireTable(table, present)
}

rule FollowerReplaysTablesRetired {
    -- Idempotent, as rule RetireTable is, so the record is never stale.
    when: msg: TablesRetiredMessage.created
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        for name in msg.table_names:
            trie_cat/RetireTable(parse_table_ref(database.name, name), msg.as_of)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Follower replay
------------------------------------------------------------

rule FollowerReplaysTriesDeleted {
    -- The follower handles TriesDeleted by removing the keys from its local trie catalog.
    --
    -- Replay is unconditionally non-stale — `trieCatalog.deleteTries` is set-removal and therefore idempotent, so even a redundant replay is harmless.
    -- (Contrast with ResolvedTx, TriesAdded, BlockBoundary and BlockUploaded, each of which has a staleness guard against the watermark that records its own effect — see db.allium "Staleness".)
    --
    -- See: indexer/FollowerLogProcessor.kt (TriesDeleted handler), api/log/ReplicaMessage.kt (TriesDeleted)
    -- Fires when a follower parses a TriesDeleted record off the replica log into a TriesDeletedMessage entity.
    when: msg: TriesDeletedMessage.created
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(
            parse_table_ref(database.name, msg.table_name),
            msg.trie_keys
        )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract TrieGcDualWriteAtomicity {
    -- Obligations on the leader that appends the trie GC's `TriesDeleted` and applies the removal it carries.
    -- These can't be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state — they are about the ordering of replica-log appends and catalog mutations across coroutines.
    -- See the long comment on rule CommitTriesDeletedRule.

    @invariant DualWriteOrdering
        -- For each (TriesDeleted append, trie catalog deleteTries) pair, no block upload — which persists a table-block snapshot of the catalog — may observe a catalog state the replica log has already moved past.
        -- Held by log order: the removal is applied on the read-back of the record that publishes it, and a block is produced on the read-back of a boundary behind that record, so every snapshot follows every removal the log already carries.

    @invariant DeleteBeforeCommit
        -- Within one (table, chunk) atomic step: every (data, meta) DELETE must succeed before the corresponding `TriesDeleted` is appended to the replica log, and so before the removal that record's read-back applies.
        -- Reversing this order would leave followers thinking deleted files are still live.

    @invariant LeaderOnly
        -- TrieGarbageCollector instances exist only inside a LeaderSystem;
        -- only one node is leader for a given database at a time. Followers
        -- never DELETE trie files or append TriesDeleted — they only react
        -- to TriesDeleted by mutating their local trie catalogs.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor AdminActor {
    -- The admin/test entry point for gcAll. Any client invoking the admin
    -- API qualifies.
    identified_by: db/Client where true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface TrieGcAdmin {
    -- The admin/test entry point. `gcAll` runs one cycle of every leader-owned
    -- collector (block + trie) and waits for both. No-op on follower nodes.
    facing caller: AdminActor

    context db: db/Database

    provides:
        AdminAwaitsNoTrieGarbage(caller, db)

    contracts:
        demands TrieGcDualWriteAtomicity

    @guarantee AwaitsFreshCycle
        -- The caller is unblocked only after a trie GC cycle that started at
        -- or after their call has completed.

    @guarantee BypassesEnabledFlag
        -- AdminAwaitsNoTrieGarbage runs a cycle regardless of config.enabled.
        -- The flag gates automatic signals from block adoption and the native term timer.

    @guarantee CycleAwaiterFairness
        -- Every caller is resumed (or cancelled) by exactly one cycle whose
        -- start time post-dates the call. The loop drains pending awaiters
        -- and signals after each cycle and re-runs if any are present.
}

-- Black box helpers used in rule expressions (resolved by the implementation,
-- not modelled in this spec):
--   chunk_by(set, n)              — partition a set into Lists of size <= n
--                                   (TrieGarbageCollector.kt)
--   data_file_path(table, key)    — table + trie_key -> ObjectStore key (data)
--                                   (storage/PathTools.kt)
--   meta_file_path(table, key)    — table + trie_key -> ObjectStore key (meta)
--                                   (storage/PathTools.kt)
--   ObjectStoreList(prefix)       — every key under a prefix (storage/ObjectStore.kt)
--   finished_groups(table, chunk) — for the cycle's first chunk of a table, the partition groups of every trie the cycle collects from it
--                                   none of whose members the catalog holds besides those tries; empty for its later chunks
--                                   (maintenance/src/gc.rs)
--   default_garbage_as_of()       — block-time threshold minus garbage_lifetime
--                                   (TrieGarbageCollector.kt: defaultGarbageAsOf)
--   parse_table_ref(db, name)     — TableRef.parse (api/.../table/TableRef.kt)
--   retired_relation_tables(t)    — the tables of owned relations due at present t (rule RetireDueTables)
--                                   (node/src/table_retirement.rs)
--   ObjectStoreDelete(path)       — DELETE one key (storage/ObjectStore.kt)

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "First-cycle contention for the persister: when an operator first flips `enabled = true` on a database that's been running without GC for months, the backlog can be enormous, and each chunk takes two turns on the leader's persister coroutine — one to append its `TriesDeleted`, one to apply the removal on reading it back — competing with source batches for select turns. Both turns are short: an unbounded-channel send on the first, a catalog set-removal on the second, the log append itself being asynchronous on the append pump. What is not bounded is the NUMBER of chunks, nor the burst of TriesDeleted the append pump then carries ahead of tx records; and a chunk completes only after a full replica-log round trip, so a cycle's cost tracks that latency as well as the backlog. Worth measuring, and considering an explicit bound on chunks per cycle or a yield between chunks."
